WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.301
Once upon a time there lived a very wealthy merchant.

2
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:11.621
He was not happy with his only son.

3
00:00:11.622 --> 00:00:17.884
The boy wasn't intelligent; he wasn't creative; and, even worse, he didn't like to work.

4
00:00:17.885 --> 00:00:22.212
His mother loved him anyway, and she always made excuses for him.

5
00:00:22.213 --> 00:00:25.851
"He's not lazy," she would say to her husband.

6
00:00:25.852 --> 00:00:32.727
"He just takes his time because he wants everything to be perfect."

7
00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:36.600
Other times she would say, "Our son is intelligent.

8
00:00:36.601 --> 00:00:41.847
He hides it from others. He doesn't want to make his friends look stupid."

9
00:00:41.848 --> 00:00:47.816
The merchant didn't believe any of it, and he would just wave his wife away.

10
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:52.872
When the son was old enough to marry, the mother begged her husband to find him a wife.

11
00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:58.236
But the merchant thought his son was too stupid, too boring, and too lazy to marry.

12
00:00:58.237 --> 00:01:03.877
The boy's mother wanted him to marry, so she went to see her husband often.

13
00:01:03.878 --> 00:01:10.190
She told her husband that she believed her son possessed a large amount of wisdom and intelligence.

14
00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:14.908
The merchant became angry with his wife, but she was too stubborn to notice.

15
00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:17.906
Finally he had had enough.

16
00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:22.913
"Look here," he said. "I have heard these stories many times.

17
00:01:22.914 --> 00:01:25.006
But you have never once proved them.

18
00:01:25.007 --> 00:01:29.267
Mothers are blind. I do not believe anything you have said.

19
00:01:29.268 --> 00:01:32.790
But it is too hot to argue. You have won.

20
00:01:32.791 --> 00:01:37.988
I will give that fool, our son, one more chance.

21
00:01:37.989 --> 00:01:42.184
"Give him this one small coin. Tell him to go to the market.

22
00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:45.544
I want him to buy a single item with the coin.

23
00:01:45.545 --> 00:01:54.462
That one item must be something to eat, something to drink, something to plant, and something to feed our goat."

24
00:01:54.463 --> 00:02:00.539
As the boy walked toward the market, he started to think, "What can I buy with this coin?

25
00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.409
Something to eat and drink and do all those other things?

26
00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:08.240
Surely this is impossible!"

27
00:02:08.241 --> 00:02:14.042
The boy sat down under a tree. He needed time to think and rest.

28
00:02:14.043 --> 00:02:17.759
As he was sitting there, the blacksmith's daughter came by.

29
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.237
She looked at the boy and saw that he was very unhappy.

30
00:02:21.238 --> 00:02:25.895
"What's the matter?" she asked.

31
00:02:25.896 --> 00:02:31.163
The boy looked at her and said, "My father has given me an impossible task.

32
00:02:31.164 --> 00:02:35.097
He wants me to buy one item with this coin.

33
00:02:35.098 --> 00:02:44.396
And that item must be something to eat, something to drink, something to plant, and something to feed our goat."

34
00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:47.560
"I know what you can do," she said.

35
00:02:47.561 --> 00:02:51.391
"Go and buy a watermelon with the coin.

36
00:02:51.392 --> 00:02:54.352
It will provide everything your father said.

37
00:02:54.353 --> 00:02:58.078
Give it to him, and your parents will be pleased."

38
00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:00.915
The boy nodded. The girl was right!

39
00:03:00.916 --> 00:03:07.639
His family could eat the watermelon, drink its juice, plant its seeds, and feed its rind to their goat.

40
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.768
"Thanks," he said.

41
00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:13.893
The boy ran to the market and got a watermelon for his father.

42
00:03:13.894 --> 00:03:18.542
When the merchant's wife saw her son's cleverness, she was very pleased.

43
00:03:18.543 --> 00:03:24.020
"Look," she said to her husband, "our son has succeeded."

44
00:03:24.021 --> 00:03:27.996
"Actually, Mom," said the boy, "I had help.

45
00:03:27.997 --> 00:03:33.217
Without the advice of the blacksmith's daughter, I would have failed."

46
00:03:33.218 --> 00:03:35.088
The father was impressed.

47
00:03:35.089 --> 00:03:41.333
His son hadn't found the solution by himself, but he was honest enough to admit it.

48
00:03:41.334 --> 00:03:43.711
Honesty was a worthy quality.

49
00:03:43.712 --> 00:03:49.226
The merchant was satisfied that his son would make a good husband because he was honest.

50
00:03:49.227 --> 00:03:52.740
He invited the blacksmith and his family for dinner.

51
00:03:52.741 --> 00:03:56.369
The families decided their son and daughter would marry.

52
00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:01.195
The blacksmith was glad that his daughter would marry into such a wealthy family.

53
00:04:01.196 --> 00:04:08.215
The merchant's son and the blacksmith's daughter were happy because they made a good team.

54
00:04:08.216 --> 00:04:11.128
The boy became a hardworking husband.

55
00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:15.232
The boy's father was glad that he had given his son another chance,

56
00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:24.428
and the boy's mother was happy because her son was wise, honest, and hardworking, just as she always knew he was.
